# Learnings — <project name>

> Transferable lessons distilled from this project's own GOTM record (`DECISIONS.md` / `audits/` / `LEDGER.md`) when the DAG drained — produced via `prompts/outcome-analysis.md`. Only *transferable* claims belong here: a stranger to this project should be able to act on each one. Project-specific detail stays in the record.
>
> **This is the cold tier, one level up.** Inside a project the cold archive feeds future *turns* — closed detail, off the hot path, pulled only on demand. This file is that same shape across the project boundary: closed-out *lessons* that feed future *drivers*. A new project's driver is born without history, exactly like a worker; the learning pool seeds it the way the store seeds a worker — letting it skip mistakes earlier drivers already paid for instead of re-discovering them at full cost. It is the cross-project analog of the store.
>
> **Two consumers, one artifact.** A future project's driver scans the **Index** (one line each), filters to the tags it's touching, and expands a record's `fix` only when relevant — cheap to read, the same hot-path discipline the archive uses. An aggregation layer (user- or enterprise-level) ingests the **Records** and merges on `claim`. See *Confidence & merge model* below, and `docs/09-learning-across-projects.md` for the full picture.

## Index — generated from the Records below

> Format: `L#  [tags]  claim → fix-gist.  (kind · confidence · grounding · source)`. This is what a future driver reads first; it expands a record only when its tags are relevant.

- `L1  [<tag>·<tag>]` <one-line claim> → <fix gist>. *(gotcha · candidate · observed · <ref>)*
- `L2  [<tag>]` <one-line claim> → <fix gist>. *(prerequisite · candidate · decided · <ref>)*

### Example retrieval
> A project touching <X> filters the index to tags `{<tag>, <tag>}` → surfaces L#, L# before starting, and expands only those. The rest never enter its driver's context.

## Records — source of truth

Schema: `id` · `claim` (the merge key) · `kind` (`gotcha` | `prerequisite` | `pivot` | `pattern` | `anti-pattern`) · `tags` (tech / domain / phase, for retrieval) · `fix` (actionable) · `scope` (where it applies) · `evidence[]` (appendable — which project, which `D##`/audit) · `confidence` (+ optional `strength`) · `grounding` (`observed` | `audited` | `decided` — how the learning was known).

```yaml
- id: <project>/L1
  claim: "<the generalized, transferable lesson — this is the merge key>"
  kind: gotcha            # gotcha | prerequisite | pivot | pattern | anti-pattern
  tags: [<tech>, <domain>, <phase>]
  fix: "<what to do instead — actionable, near copy-pasteable>"
  scope: <where this applies>
  evidence:
    - {project: <project>, ref: <D## or audit Uxx>, note: "<what was observed>"}
  confidence: candidate   # candidate (1 project) → validated (≥2 independent) → core
  grounding: observed     # observed (a LEDGER revert/pivot/supersession) | audited (independent FAIL→fix verdict) | decided (a DECISIONS.md choice, may be untested)
  strength: "<within-project weight, e.g. '1 decision' or 'recurred ~6 units'>"

# - id: <project>/L2
#   ... one block per learning ...
```

## Confidence & merge model

- **Ladder.** `candidate` (seen in 1 project) → `validated` (≥2 *independent* projects confirm the same `claim`) → `core` (enterprise-curated, broadly applicable). A project can't mint `validated` on its own — that needs independent confirmation (the *auditor ≠ author* principle, lifted across the project boundary).
- **Strength** weights a candidate by recurrence *within* this project; it never substitutes for cross-project confirmation. A lesson the audits flagged across many units is a sturdy candidate — still a candidate.
- **Grounding** is the *within-record evidence axis* (Axis B): how the learning was known — `observed`/`audited` outweigh `decided` on a tie (reality corrected the agent, vs. an untested choice). It complements the candidate→validated *count* axis (Axis B's cross-record side) rather than replacing it. Absent = `decided`/unknown (back-compat); the merge key stays `claim`.
- **Merge.** Match incoming records on `claim`; on a match, **append** the new `evidence` and recompute `confidence` — the second project doesn't create a duplicate, it ticks the existing record up. The record grows; the pool does not bloat. On a **contradiction** (a later project reports the opposite outcome), don't overwrite — flag for review and demote. That demotion path is the decay that keeps the pool from rotting into stale, confidently-wrong advice.
